WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:04.591
My name is Fillippo Bogliano, and I live in northern Italy.

2
00:00:04.592 --> 00:00:10.931
My home is on the southern slopes of the Alps in a city called Turin, which is also on the Po River.

3
00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:20.359
Many people travel here to trade with the local merchants for all kinds of products; some buy red wine while others come for our nutty chocolate.

4
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.701
But Turin is best known for its cheese! How do I know this?

5
00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:29.629
Because I am a casaro , an expert cheese

6
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:36.075
maker. My family first came to this area from France in 1745, when I was just a year

7
00:00:36.076 --> 00:00:43.829
old. Then my father herded cattle in the mountains and sold fresh milk, but this was hard work and did not pay very

8
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:49.253
well. My father soon discovered that the casaros who bought his milk made a very nice

9
00:00:49.254 --> 00:00:54.134
living. So he decided that when I was old enough, I would learn the cheese-making

10
00:00:54.135 --> 00:01:00.664
trade. When I reached the age of 11, my father sent me to live with one of the most respected casaros in

11
00:01:00.665 --> 00:01:06.699
Turin. This man was glad to be my mentor since my father had taught me so much about milk

12
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.973
already. I was an apprentice for five years, and I learned everything there was to know about making

13
00:01:12.974 --> 00:01:21.078
cheese. I quickly realized that no two batches of cheese are alike, just as no pail of fresh milk is exactly the same as

14
00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:28.350
another. I also learned that even a small change in the cheese-making process can result in a completely different kind of

15
00:01:28.351 --> 00:01:34.116
cheese. Now I am 22, and I have my own business, which keeps my whole family

16
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:42.382
busy. My mother and father sell our cheese in the marketplace; my two brothers tend the cows, and my wife, Magda, manages the

17
00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:46.380
household. We all work together because making cheese is difficult

18
00:01:46.381 --> 00:01:52.094
work. Several kinds of cheese are made in this region of Italy, but Gorgonzola is my

19
00:01:52.095 --> 00:01:57.538
favorite, because this white, creamy cheese with blue veins has a sharp, tangy

20
00:01:57.539 --> 00:02:02.099
flavor. It is the most famous cheese in Turin and my

21
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:09.987
specialty. Legend has it that Gorgonzola was discovered by accident by an apprentice casaro who was very much in

22
00:02:09.988 --> 00:02:14.603
love. He was late for a meeting with his girlfriend, but he had not finished his

23
00:02:14.604 --> 00:02:20.162
work. So he dumped his unfinished cheese into a pail of fresh milk that a herdsman had brought

24
00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:28.543
in. Later the mixture hardened, became moldy, and turned greenish blue, but the casaro didn't want to throw it

25
00:02:28.544 --> 00:02:35.416
away. So he sold it anyway, and his customers loved it! Gorgonzola cheese was

26
00:02:35.417 --> 00:02:40.413
born. To be honest, though, making this wonderful cheese takes talent and

27
00:02:40.414 --> 00:02:48.570
patience. Every evening, after our herd comes down from the alpine meadows, my brothers milk the cows and bring the milk into my

28
00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:53.797
workshop. I carefully pour the milk into shallow pails and allow it to set

29
00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:58.006
overnight. The next day I add a special ingredient called

30
00:02:58.007 --> 00:03:03.703
rennet. Rennet is an acidic enzyme, a natural chemical produced in a calf's

31
00:03:03.704 --> 00:03:11.786
stomach. It turns the milk into a curd—a thickened milk paste—and separates the curd from the whey, a thin and yellowish liquid.

32
00:03:11.787 --> 00:03:20.258
In fact, this action is called "turning" the milk. On the morning of the second day, I make another curd from the morning milking.

33
00:03:20.259 --> 00:03:25.952
Then, in the afternoon, I combine the curd from the first day with the curd from the second day.

34
00:03:25.953 --> 00:03:31.792
I put the fresh curd on the bottom and sides of the pail and the older curd in the center.

35
00:03:31.793 --> 00:03:38.608
Next I gently knead the curd with my hands, smoothing it out, and remove it from the pail.

36
00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:42.955
Then I shape the cheese into a circular shape, called a round.

37
00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:52.835
Many types of cheese are made in this way, and cheese can be made in rounds of any size—some are small and some are quite large.

38
00:03:52.836 --> 00:04:01.374
I make about three rounds a day, and flip them over frequently to drain the remaining whey out of the curd.

39
00:04:01.375 --> 00:04:08.822
Most days there is no time to go back to the house for breakfast or lunch, so Magda brings pasta to my workshop.

40
00:04:08.823 --> 00:04:14.013
She doesn't like to come here very much because she thinks that the cheese is too stinky.

41
00:04:14.014 --> 00:04:17.866
She says that Gorgonzola smells like dirty socks!

42
00:04:17.867 --> 00:04:20.910
She is right; it does smell pretty bad.

43
00:04:20.911 --> 00:04:24.689
But I am so used to it now that it doesn't bother me.

44
00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.515
I just think about how good it will taste.

45
00:04:29.516 --> 00:04:36.407
The final step is to add sea salt to the rounds of curd over a period of five days to firm them up.

46
00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:42.607
Then the rounds are ready to be ripened, and my brothers and I take all the rounds to a nearby cave.

47
00:04:42.608 --> 00:04:49.386
We stack them on tall wooden racks like books on a library shelf, and as they go through the aging process,

48
00:04:49.387 --> 00:04:53.167
I return to the cave often to flip the rounds over.

49
00:04:53.168 --> 00:04:56.915
This keeps the rounds firm and hard as they age.

50
00:04:56.916 --> 00:05:03.015
Our caves in northern Italy have the perfect temperature and humidity for ripening the cheese.

51
00:05:03.016 --> 00:05:07.355
These special caves also have a mold that grows in them.

52
00:05:07.356 --> 00:05:17.204
It attaches itself to the ripening cheese and, like magic, causes the blue and green veins to appear in the cheese in about three weeks!

53
00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:22.836
We have to let the Gorgonzola ripen for at least three months before it is ready to eat.

54
00:05:22.837 --> 00:05:30.976
Younger cheese tastes sweeter and is softer. Rounds that are aged more than six months are harder and have a spicy flavor.

55
00:05:30.977 --> 00:05:37.453
Older Gorgonzola that is aged for a year is more valuable, so we mostly make this kind.

56
00:05:37.454 --> 00:05:45.404
When the cheese rounds are ready, my brothers carry them to the wagon so that my mother and father can take them to the market the next day.

57
00:05:45.405 --> 00:05:54.716
At our stall, Bogliano's Gorgonzola, everyone can stop and taste our cheese, drink a little wine, and talk about the lovely weather.

58
00:05:54.717 --> 00:06:03.782
Oh, I wish I could eat and talk with our customers, but I must stay behind to clean up the caves and watch over the next batch of cheese as it ages.

59
00:06:03.783 --> 00:06:09.828
My cheese-making work never ends, but I love it!

